    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "The study shows that experiences of sexual violence, sexual harassment, and sexual abuse in sport are all positively associated with disordered eating in adolescent athletes, independent of gender, sport type, and sport level. These findings highlight the importance of screening for both disordered eating and sexual violence, as well as implementing prevention strategies targeting the broader sport environment"
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The cross-sectional design prevents causal inference, and the use of a convenience sample and self-reported measures limits generalizability and may introduce bias. Additionally, simplified categorizations (e.g., sport level, sport type) may not fully capture the complexity of athletes’ experiences"
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future research should use longitudinal designs to clarify causal directions and explore additional risk, protective, and mediating factors (e.g., social support, coping). More nuanced measures (e.g., sport level, type of sport, perpetrator characteristics) and contextual influences should also be examined to better understand and prevent disordered eating in athletes"
    },
